  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+8x+11.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=8,$ and $c=11.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{8}{2\\cdot 2}=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}+8x+11 \\\\  & =2(-2)^{2}+8(-2)+11 \\\\  & =8-16+11 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-2,3),$ and the range of the function is $[3,\\infty ),$ i.e. $3\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/3a87830f59bf840ac8ab0d761ab7319b53fc804bc9d925e92850313d5d5a7a52.png)",
